"[2610.23071] Before Retrieval Begins: Safety and Tool-Use Decisions in LLM Agents (https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.23071)\nciteturn41301view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.23071\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 164\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[2610.22631] A Plausible Explanation Is Enough to Mislead Multimodal Judges (https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.22631)\nciteturn41301view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.22631\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 164\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA Plausible Explanation Is Enough to Mislead Multimodal Judges (https://arxiv.org/html/2610.22631v1)\nciteturn41301view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn41300view2\",\"lineno\":236}); Total lines: 1082\nL176: Judge  | Acc.  | CRR $\\uparrow$  | IRR $\\downarrow$\nL177:  |  | NoExp  | Plain  | CoT  | Pers.  | NoExp  | Plain  | CoT  | Pers.\nL178: Random Predictor  | 50.0  | 50.0  | 50.0  | 50.0  | 50.0  | 50.0  | 50.0  | 50.0  | 50.0\nL179: Closed-Source Models\nL180: ---\nL181: Gemini 3 Flash  | 73.9  | 40.6  | 33.0  | 30.2  | 30.9  | 6.1  | 7.1  | 3.6  | 4.8\nL184: GPT-5.4  | 58.9  | 35.4  | 27.1  | 27.1  | 28.5  | 9.5  | 8.3  | 8.3  | 8.2\nL185: GPT-5.4 mini  | 52.2  | 17.7  | 13.5  | 13.5  | 13.5  | 7.1  | 4.8  | 2.4  | 3.4\nL186: GPT-5.6 Luna  | 47.2  | 28.1  | 38.5  | 34.4  | 39.3  | 22.6  | 16.7  | 21.4  | 21.0\nL187: Open-Source Models\nL188: ---\nL189: InternVL3.5-14B  | 50.0  | 33.3  | 28.4  | 25.0  | 27.3  | 22.6  | 12.0  | 13.1  | 12.7\nL190: InternVL3.5-8B  | 42.8  | 41.7  | 10.4  | 12.5  | 12.7  | 19.0  | 4.8  | 6.0  | 4.3\nL191: Qwen3-VL-2B  | 49.4  | 66.7  | 27.1  | 34.4  | 33.6  | 60.7  | 23.8  | 22.6  | 22.0\nL192: Qwen3-VL-8B  | 55.6  | 40.6  | 17.7  | 12.5  | 15.5  | 20.7  | 4.8  | 2.4  | 3.8\nL193: Qwen3-VL-32B  | 50.0  | 43.8  | 26.0  | 18.8  | 21.3  | 27.4  | 9.5  | 8.3  | 12.2\nL194: Gemma 3-4B  | 38.3  | 55.4  | 38.5  | 40.6  | 38.0  | 46.3  | 35.7  | 33.7  | 31.1\nL195: Gemma 3-12B  | 40.6  | 46.9  | 21.9  | 27.1  | 27.5  | 28.6  | 10.7  | 7.1  | 11.8\nL196: Gemma 3-27B  | 40.6  | 40.6  | 30.2  | 32.3  | 31.2  | 33.3  | 17.9  | 9.5  | 16.4\nL197: Mean  | 52.1  | 42.4  | 29.7  | 28.6  | 29.9  | 23.3  | 12.6  | 11.7  | 12.6\nL198: Table 2: Judge model performance across explanation conditions. Acc. is the model’s accuracy on VQA questions. Correct Rejection Rate (CRR %) and Incorrect Rejection Rate (IRR %) are reported as absolute values for each condition: no explanation (NoExp), plain explanation (Plain), chain-of-thought (CoT), and persuasive explanation (Pers.). The darker shades of Blue or Red indicate larger rejection rate.\nL199: #### Evaluation setup.\nL200: We evaluate 14 MLLM judges from five model families: eight open-source models ranging from 2B to 32B parameters and six proprietary models. Each judge follows the reference-free prompt in §cite8†2.1 , outputs a four-point ordinal label, which is binarized into $D\\in\\{\\textsc{reject},\\textsc{accept}\\}$. The full judge list, prompts, and hyperparameters appear in Appendix cite45†A . Unless otherwise noted, evaluations use the main set.\nL201: We aggregate Persuasion over both variants and all 41 techniques, yielding 82 explanations per query; §cite27†3.3 analyzes variants and techniques.\nL202: #### Metrics.\nL203: \nL204: Let $D_{i}\\in\\{\\textsc{reject},\\textsc{accept}\\}$ be the binary decision for item $i$. We compute CRR on incorrect-candidate items $\\mathcal{I}^{-}$ and IRR on correct-candidate items $\\mathcal{I}^{+}$:\nL205: \nL206:  | $\\displaystyle\\mathrm{CRR}$  | $\\displaystyle=\\Pr(D_{i}=\\textsc{reject}\\mid i\\in\\mathcal{I}^{-}),$  |  | (2)\nL207:  | $\\displaystyle\\mathrm{IRR}$  | $\\displaystyle=\\Pr(D_{i}=\\textsc{reject}\\mid i\\in\\mathcal{I}^{+}).$  |\nL208: CRR measures correct rejection of wrong answers, while IRR measures false rejection of correct answers. We also analyze four-label verdict distributions to capture finer shifts in judge behavior. We discuss metric design in Appendix cite63†B.4 .\nL209: We further analyze each judge’s decisions using signal detection theory (SDT) (cite110†Stanislaw and Todorov, 1999 ), treating correct candidates as signals and acceptance as a “yes” response. With a hit rate of $1-\\mathrm{IRR}$ and a false-alarm rate of $1-\\mathrm{CRR}$, we quantify discriminability as $d^{\\prime}=z(\\mathrm{CRR})-z(\\mathrm{IRR})$ and the decision criterion as $c=\\frac{1}{2}[z(\\mathrm{CRR})+z(\\mathrm{IRR})]$. Here, $z$ is the standard normal quantile function.\nL210: Higher $d^{\\prime}$ indicates better discrimination, while lower $c$ indicates greater willingness to accept an answer.\nL211: Figure 2: Judgment behavior relative to no explanation. (a) Decision-criterion shift ($\\Delta c$) and (b) discriminability change ($\\Delta d^{\\prime}$) per judge (dots), with the cross-judge mean (diamonds) and item-level bootstrap 95% CI. (c) Four-label verdict shares on incorrect-candidate items, averaged over the 14 judges.\nL212: #### Research Questions.\nL213: \nL214: We organize the evaluation around four questions: RQ1 whether plausible explanations degrade judge reliability (§cite19†3.1 ); RQ2 how they shift judges’ decisions and what drives the shift (§cite22†3.2 ); RQ3 which factors modulate plausibility capture (§cite27†3.3 ); and RQ4 whether plausibility capture generalizes across deployment settings (§cite33†3.4 ). We also test mitigations (§cite38†3.5 ).\nL215: ### 3.1 Effects on Judge Reliability\nL216: \nL217: Table cite111†2 reports CRR on incorrect-candidate items and IRR on correct-candidate items for 14 judges under four explanation conditions.\nL218: #### Ordinary plausible explanations and deliberate persuasion produce similar average rejection declines.\nL219: On incorrect-candidate items, plausible explanations reduce rejection rate by comparable margins: 12.7 pp under Plain, 13.8 pp under CoT, and 12.5 pp under Persuasion. Persuasion is thus no more effective than a plain justification on average. Open-source judges show larger effects (20 pp on average under each condition), whereas four of the six proprietary judges drop by 4-13 pp and the other two rise under all three conditions: Gemini 3.1 Pro and GPT-5.6 Luna.\nL220: We discuss these two exceptions further in §cite22†3.2 .\nL221: #### The shift reaches confident rejections.\nL222: The share of “definitely incorrect” verdicts falls from 26.9% without explanation to 16.5-18.7% with plausible explanations (Figure cite112†2 c). Of the candidates a judge labels “definitely incorrect” without explanation, 36-39% are accepted when the same candidate comes with a plausible explanation, and 23% are labeled “definitely correct”, compared with 10% and 7% under Vacuous, which shares the prompt format and randomized option order but offers no answer support.\nL223: ### 3.2 How Explanations Shift Judgments\nL224: #### Explanations also reduce rejection of correct answers.\nL225: On correct-candidate items, plausible explanations also lower rejection rate, on average by 10.7 pp under Plain, 11.6 pp under CoT, and 10.7 pp under Persuasion, with declines observed in 12-13 of the 14 judges. As with CRR, the change is concentrated in open-source judges, which start from a far higher IRR (32.3% vs. 11.3%). The similar CRR and IRR declines raise a question: do explanations improve discrimination, or change the criterion used to judge answers?\nL226: Figure cite112†2 decomposes this shift: signal-detection criterion and discriminability (a-b) and four-label verdict shares on incorrect-candidate items (c). Per-judge values, label shares, and verdict transitions are in Appendix cite70†C.2 (Tables cite113†8 and cite114†9 , Figure cite115†8 ).\nL227: #### Judgment criteria shift without a clear mean change in discriminability.\nL228: \nL229: All three explanation conditions shift the mean criterion toward acceptance by similar amounts ($\\Delta c=-0.39$, $-0.45$, and $-0.39$ under Plain, CoT, and Persuasion; 95% CIs exclude zero), with this direction observed in 12 of 14 judges (Figure cite112†2 a). Mean $\\Delta d^{\\prime}$ ranges from $+0.04$ to $+0.09$, with CIs spanning zero; discriminability rises for 7-8 judges and falls for the rest (Figure cite112†2 b).\nL230: #### Overall accuracy can conceal worse error detection.\nL231: \nL232: Across 14 judges, judgment accuracy drops by less than 2 pp (58.3% to 56.4-56.7%) while CRR falls by 12.5-13.8 pp. For Gemma 3-27B, accuracy rises by 1.7-6.7 pp despite an 8.3-10.4 pp CRR decline. More frequent acceptance of correct answers can thus mask increased acceptance of wrong ones (Appendix cite63†B.4 ).\nL233: #### Controls suggest a role for item-specific answer support.\nL234: Vacuous explanations, which add explanation-shaped text without item-specific content, produce a small shift toward rejection ($\\Delta c=+0.08$, 95% CI $[+0.03,+0.12]$), opposite to plausible explanations. Mismatch explanations shift all 14 judges toward rejection (mean $\\Delta c=+0.95$; Figure cite112†2 a) and raise IRR by 30.7 pp (Appendix cite70†C.2 , Table cite116†7 ). These contrasts suggest that item-specific answer support contributes to the criterion shift.\nL235: The two exceptions in §cite19†3.1 , Gemini 3.1 Pro and GPT-5.6 Luna, differ from their own Vacuous criterion by at most 0.07 under plausible explanations, versus 0.12-1.01 for the other judges. Pro responds little to explanation content, whereas Luna gains discriminability; Luna nonetheless shows capture on the independent dataset in §cite33†3.4 . Appendix cite71†C.2 details both cases.\nL236: Figure 3: Each of the 41 persuasion techniques under VI and TO, by mean persuasive-rhetoric rating and mean CRR drop from no explanation over the 14 judges. Plain and CoT are condition-level references.\nL237: ### 3.3 Factors Modulating Plausibility Capture\nL238: \nL239: Figure 4: Per-judge CRR on the induced-wrong set and VQA accuracy on the same items (diamonds).\nL240: \nL241: Figures cite117†3 -cite118†5 examine persuasion techniques, visual access during explanation generation and judging, and the judge’s ability to answer the question.\nL242: #### More persuasive rhetoric does not imply greater capture.\nL243: Three LLM raters (Qwen3-VL-32B, DeepSeek-V4-Flash, and GPT-5.4 mini) score persuasive rhetoric beyond evidence description on a 1-5 scale, seeing only the question and explanation (Appendix cite69†C.1 ). The techniques add rhetoric: mean ratings range from 1.84 to 2.97 under VI and from 1.86 to 2.96 under TO, all above Plain (1.52) and CoT (1.51; Figure cite117†3 ). Yet across the 41 techniques, rhetoric ratings are uncorrelated with CRR drops (Spearman $\\rho=0.00$ under both variants).\nL244: Even the highest- and lowest-rated techniques, Threats and Information-dense, lower CRR by similar margins (15.2 and 14.4 pp under VI; Appendix cite58†B.2 , Table cite119†4 ).\nL245: #### Technique effects are inconsistent across judges and persuasion variants.\nL246: \nL247: Judges agree poorly on which techniques work best: ICC(3,1) across the 41 techniques is 0.14 for VI and 0.16 for TO, both poor under Cicchetti’s guidelines ($<.40$) (cite120†Cicchetti, 1994 ). Rankings also change between variants, with a median within-judge Spearman correlation of 0.24 between VI and TO technique effects.\nL248: #### Plausibility capture persists under text-only rewriting.\nL249: \nL250: Persuasion-TO rewrites an image-informed CoT explanation, while VI additionally gives the generator the image (§cite11†2.3 ). Judges see the image in both conditions, so this comparison tests additional visual access during rewriting. Mean CRR differs by just 0.5 pp (29.6% VI vs. 30.1% TO), and per-judge changes are highly correlated ($r\\geq 0.998$ for both CRR and IRR; Appendix cite70†C.2 , Table cite116†7 ).\nL251: #### Visual evidence contributes to discrimination across explanation conditions.\nL252: \nL253: Replacing images with blank inputs for 11 judges reduces $d^{\\prime}$ in 40 of 44 judge-condition cells across NoExp, Plain, CoT, and Persuasion-VI. Figure cite118†5 shows the corresponding CRR-IRR gap. Explanation effects thus coexist with a measurable benefit from visual input (Appendix cite78†D.2 ).\nL254: Figure 5: Judge reliability with real images (y-axis) versus blank inputs (x-axis), across 11 judges and four explanation conditions. Panels show CRR, IRR, and their gap; points above the diagonal in (c) indicate a larger gap with real images.\nL255: #### Plausibility capture persists even when judges can answer the questions.\nL256: On the induced-wrong set, judges answer the questions directly with a mean accuracy of 93.2% and reject the intentionally wrong candidate answers at a high rate without explanations, with a mean CRR of 84%. Nevertheless, as shown in Figure cite121†4 (per-judge values in Appendix cite70†C.2 , Table cite116†7 ), Persuasion explanations still lower CRR to 77% with drops for 11 of the 14 judges.\nL257: Even the most accurate judges are captured: GPT-5.4 mini (98.5% accuracy) and Gemini 3.1 Flash-Lite (100%) still lose 7.2 and 6.2 pp of CRR.\nL258: ### 3.4 Generalization Across Settings\nL259:  | CRR $\\uparrow$  | IRR $\\downarrow$  | $\\rho$\nL260: Setting  | NoExp  | $\\Delta$Plain  | $\\Delta$Pers.  | NoExp  | $\\Delta$Plain  | $\\Delta$Pers.  | Plain  | Pers.\nL261: In-distribution (main set)\nL262: All  | 42.4  | $-$12.7  | $-$12.5  | 23.3  | $-$10.7  | $-$10.7  | ref  | ref\nL263: Chart  | 43.8  | $-$8.2  | $-$11.1  | 25.0  | $-$12.0  | $-$12.7  | -  | -\nL264: Medical  | 36.5  | $-$9.4  | $-$10.4  | 24.8  | $-$11.0  | $-$11.7  | -  | -\nL265: Hallucination  | 50.9  | $-$20.9  | $-$16.9  | 20.1  | $-$9.3  | $-$7.6  | -  | -\nL266: Out-of-distribution\nL267: Grounding  | 73.0  | $-$15.5  | $-$11.6  | 13.7  | $-$9.7  | $-$7.6  | 0.77  | 0.76\nL268: Explanation generator\nL269: Gemini expl.  | 42.4  | $-$12.4  | -  | 23.3  | $-$9.7  | -  | 0.90  | -\nL270: Image quality\nL271: Clean  | 40.9  | $-$15.7  | $-$16.3  | 22.4  | $-$10.8  | $-$11.8  | ref  | ref\nL272: JPEG  | 44.4  | $-$18.2  | $-$19.3  | 24.4  | $-$12.1  | $-$12.8  | 0.83  | 0.98\nL273: Noise  | 42.5  | $-$18.1  | $-$18.7  | 23.6  | $-$11.5  | $-$11.7  | 0.93  | 0.98\nL274: Table 3: Plausibility capture across deployment settings. NoExp: mean CRR and IRR without explanation (%); $\\Delta$Plain and $\\Delta$Pers.: paired change (pp) under Plain and Persuasion. $\\rho$: Spearman correlation of per-judge CRR drops with the ref row. All rows use 14 judges except Grounding (12) and image quality (9).\nL275: Table cite122†3 and Figure cite123†7 test transfer across datasets, explanation generators, image quality, and judging protocols. On the main set, Plain and Persuasion lower CRR and IRR in all three domains. Appendix cite72†D details joint answer/explanation generation, visual annotations, multi-turn context, and multi-criteria judging.\nL276: \nL277: Figure 6: CRR (a) and IRR (b) under Persuasion-VI, alone or with conservative judging or self-verification.\nL278: #### Plausibility capture transfers across generators and datasets.\nL279: \nL280: We use a lightweight proprietary model, Gemini 3 Flash, to regenerate the main set’s Plain explanations. CRR falls by 12.4 pp on average, similar to the 12.7 pp drop with GPT-5.4 mini. Judges with larger rejection-rate drops from GPT-5.4 mini’s explanations also tend to show larger drops from Gemini 3 Flash’s explanations (Spearman’s $\\rho=0.90$).\nL281: We also apply the JUPE explanation conditions to an out-of-distribution domain, visual grounding, on a set built from GQA (cite124†Hudson and Manning, 2019 ), with explanations generated by Grok 4.6. The 120 queries yield 240 items, balanced between correct and incorrect candidates. As Table cite122†3 shows, explanations still lower judges’ CRR under Plain (by 15.5 pp) and Persuasion (by 11.6 pp). CRR drops correlate across the two sets ($\\rho=0.77$ for Plain; $0.76$ for Persuasion).\nL282: #### Marking the candidate object can further sway judgments.\nL283: On the grounding set, Plain+Box adds a red box around the object named by the candidate answer, using manually verified annotations. Mean CRR falls from 57.5% under Plain to 50.3%, a further 7.2 pp decline, compared with 73.0% without explanations. The 22.7 pp decline from no explanation has a 95% CI of $[18.3,27.2]$ pp under query-level bootstrapping. Relative to Plain, CRR falls in nine of 12 judges; mean IRR changes from 4.0% to 3.6% (Appendix cite72†D , Table cite125†10 ).\nL284: The box locates the candidate object even for incorrect answers.\nL285: #### Plausibility capture is stable under image degradation.\nL286: We evaluate how common image transformations affect plausibility capture by applying two perturbations: JPEG compression and additive Gaussian noise. As shown in Table cite122†3 , explanations still lower CRR by 18-19 pp and IRR by 11-13 pp relative to no explanation, close to the 16 pp and 11-12 pp drops for the same judges on clean images. The judges most affected on clean images are also the most affected on degraded ones (Spearman’s $\\rho=0.83$-$0.98$ for per-judge CRR drops).\nL287: Figure 7: Mean normalized ratings of incorrect candidates by judging protocol, displayed as percentages (main: Output scale, 4-label). Stems connect no-explanation means (grey) to explanation means; dots show variation across judges.\nL288: #### Plausibility capture holds across judging protocols.\nL289: We replace the four-label scale with numeric scales of 0-1, 0-3, and 0-10, and separately ask judges to reason before giving a verdict. Dividing each rating by its scale maximum yields a normalized rating, rather than a binary acceptance rate. Under these protocols, explanations raise mean normalized ratings of incorrect candidates by 0.08-0.15, compared with about 0.14 under the main protocol (Figure cite123†7 ).\nL290: Merging the answer and explanation into one open-ended response increases this effect to 0.20-0.23, despite similar no-explanation ratings. Correct candidates also receive larger rating increases in this format (Appendix cite79†D.3 ).\nL291: ### 3.5 Can Plausibility Capture Be Mitigated?\nL292: #### Prompting can improve rejection of incorrect answers.\nL293: We evaluate five prompt-level mitigations on 10 judges under Persuasion-VI (Figure cite126†6 ; Appendix cite81†E , Table cite127†18 ). The two strongest methods instruct judges to decide conservatively (Conservative instruction) or answer the question before judging (Self-verification). They increase wrong-answer rejection by 25% and 13%, respectively, relative to explanations alone. Correct-answer acceptance increases by 11% and 12%, respectively, relative to the no-explanation baseline.\nL294: Effects vary by judge: Conservative instruction raises CRR by 22.7 pp for Gemma 3-12B, exceeding its no-explanation baseline, but lowers it by 1.0 pp for InternVL3.5-8B. These differences call for model-specific validation.\nL295: ## 4 Related Work\nL296: #### Biases of (M)LLM judges.\nL297: (M)LLM judges serve as raters in evaluation and reward pipelines (cite95†Zheng et al., 2023 ; cite128†Liu et al., 2023 ; cite129†Kim et al., 2024 ; cite90†Lambert et al., 2025 ), yet exhibit biases (cite96†Ye et al., 2025 ) related to position, verbosity, and self-enhancement (cite95†Zheng et al., 2023 ; cite130†Liusie et al., 2024 ), as well as stated recency or source (cite131†Marioriyad et al., 2025 ).\nL298: Multimodal studies document language overreliance (cite132†Zheng et al., 2025 ), preferences for more informative but visually inconsistent answers (cite133†Zou et al., 2026 ), and visually inconsistent judgments despite correct image perception (cite134†Park et al., 2026 ).\nL299: #### Explanations and persuasion in judging.\nL300: Content-independent adversarial phrases inflate scores irrespective of answer quality (cite135†Raina et al., 2024 ), embedded persuasion raises scores for wrong solutions (cite97†Hwang et al., 2025 ), and fluent reasoning can mislead even strong judges (cite98†Tu et al., 2026 ). Plausibility capture also arises without the user pressure studied in sycophancy (cite136†Sharma et al., 2024 ; cite137†Hong et al., 2025 ; cite138†Kim and Khashabi, 2025 ). Our controls suggest a role for item-specific answer support.\nL301: With the image, question, and answer fixed, we observe similar average judgment shifts under ordinary plausible explanations and deliberate persuasion, and distinguish criterion shifts from changes in discriminability using SDT.\nL302: ## 5 Conclusion\nL303: JUPE holds candidate answers fixed while varying their explanations in VQA-style judging. Across 14 MLLM judges, we identify plausibility capture: plausible explanations shift judgments toward acceptance of both correct and incorrect answers on average, without a clear change in mean discriminability. Plain justifications and CoT produce shifts comparable to dedicated persuasive arguments, showing that capture does not require explicit persuasive rhetoric.\nL304: The vacuous and mismatched controls suggest a role for item-specific answer support. Capture can occur even when judges answer the questions correctly themselves. Image ablations confirm that visual input still aids discrimination, while boxes marking candidate objects can further sway judgments.\nL305: The effect persists across datasets, explanation generators, and judging protocols. The strongest prompt-level mitigations improve mean wrong-answer rejection, with effects varying across judges. These findings highlight risks in reference-free evaluation of answer-explanation outputs: over-accepting incorrect answers and inflating performance estimates. Evaluation should report judgments on correct and incorrect answers separately, together with criterion and discriminability.\nL306: ## Limitations\nL307: \nL308: Our study examines plausibility capture in a controlled evaluation setting. We hold candidate answers and their correctness fixed while varying the accompanying explanations, so that changes in judgments can be associated with explanation conditions. The reported effect sizes describe these comparisons and vary across judges, datasets, and protocols. We report aggregate trends together with per-judge results to make this variation explicit and contextualize capture within each setting.\nL309: ## Ethics Statement\nL310: This work studies reliability risks in MLLM-as-judge systems. JUPE uses publicly released open-source VQA-style datasets and follows their licenses and terms of use; no private or restricted data is used. We evaluate open-source models under their respective licenses and access commercial models only through authorized interfaces. We do not extract model parameters, bypass access controls, or use model outputs to train competing models.\nL311: Because explanation-based judging could be misused to craft misleading rationales, we report aggregate findings to support safer evaluation.\nL312: ## References\nL313:   * Chen et al. (2024) D. Chen, R. Chen, S. Zhang, Y. Wang, Y. Liu, H. Zhou, Q. Zhang, Y. Wan, P. Zhou, and L. Sun MLLM-as-a-judge: assessing multimodal LLM-as-a-judge with vision-language benchmark. In Proceedings of the 41st International Conference on Machine Learning, R. Salakhutdinov, Z. Kolter, K. Heller, A. Weller, N. Oliver, J. Scarlett, and F. Berkenkamp (Eds.), Proceedings of Machine Learning Research, Vol. 235, pp. 6562–6595. External Links: cite139†Link†proceedings.mlr.press Cited by: 